## 3. Hosted Zones

### What is a Hosted Zone

A hosted zone is a container for DNS records for a specific domain. When you create a hosted zone in Route 53, Route 53 automatically creates a set of 4 nameservers.

### Create Hosted Zone

```bash
# Create hosted zone
ZONE=$(aws route53 create-hosted-zone \
  --name example.com \
  --caller-reference $(date +%s) \
  --hosted-zone-config Comment="Production zone" \
  --query 'HostedZone.Id' \
  --output text)

echo "Hosted Zone ID: $ZONE"

# Extract Zone ID
ZONE_ID=$(echo $ZONE | cut -d/ -f3)
echo "Zone ID: $ZONE_ID"

# Get nameservers
aws route53 get-hosted-zone \
  --id $ZONE_ID \
  --query 'DelegationSet.NameServers' \
  --output text

# Output:
# ns-123.awsdns-12.com
# ns-456.awsdns-34.org
# ns-789.awsdns-56.net
# ns-000.awsdns-78.co.uk
```

### Nameserver Configuration

When you create a hosted zone, you must update your domain registrar (GoDaddy, Namecheap, etc.) with Route 53's nameservers:

```
1. Copy Route 53 nameservers
2. Go to your registrar (where you bought domain)
3. Update nameservers to Route 53's nameservers
4. Wait for DNS propagation (24-48 hours typically)
5. Verify DNS resolution
```

### List Hosted Zones

```bash
# List all hosted zones
aws route53 list-hosted-zones \
  --query 'HostedZones[*].[Name,Id,Config.Comment]' \
  --output table

# Output:
# Name           | Id              | Comment
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# example.com.   | Z1234567890ABC  | Production
# test.com.      | Z9876543210XYZ  | Staging
```

