## 5. Routing Policies

### 1. Simple Routing

Returns single IP address for domain.

```bash
aws route53 change-resource-record-sets \
  --hosted-zone-id $ZONE_ID \
  --change-batch '{
    "Changes": [{
      "Action": "CREATE",
      "ResourceRecordSet": {
        "Name": "example.com",
        "Type": "A",
        "TTL": 300,
        "ResourceRecords": [{"Value": "203.0.113.42"}]
      }
    }]
  }'

# All users → 203.0.113.42
# No failover, no load balancing
# Good for single server
```

### 2. Weighted Routing

Distributes traffic by weight percentage.

```bash
# 70% traffic to Server A
aws route53 change-resource-record-sets \
  --hosted-zone-id $ZONE_ID \
  --change-batch '{
    "Changes": [{
      "Action": "CREATE",
      "ResourceRecordSet": {
        "Name": "example.com",
        "Type": "A",
        "SetIdentifier": "Server-A-70percent",
        "Weight": 70,
        "TTL": 300,
        "ResourceRecords": [{"Value": "203.0.113.1"}]
      }
    }]
  }'

# 30% traffic to Server B
aws route53 change-resource-record-sets \
  --hosted-zone-id $ZONE_ID \
  --change-batch '{
    "Changes": [{
      "Action": "CREATE",
      "ResourceRecordSet": {
        "Name": "example.com",
        "Type": "A",
        "SetIdentifier": "Server-B-30percent",
        "Weight": 30,
        "TTL": 300,
        "ResourceRecords": [{"Value": "203.0.113.2"}]
      }
    }]
  }'

# Use Case: Canary deployments (90% old version, 10% new)
# Use Case: A/B testing (50% version A, 50% version B)
```

### 3. Latency-Based Routing

Routes to region with lowest latency.

```bash
# Route to US East
aws route53 change-resource-record-sets \
  --hosted-zone-id $ZONE_ID \
  --change-batch '{
    "Changes": [{
      "Action": "CREATE",
      "ResourceRecordSet": {
        "Name": "example.com",
        "Type": "A",
        "SetIdentifier": "US-East",
        "Region": "us-east-1",
        "TTL": 300,
        "ResourceRecords": [{"Value": "203.0.113.1"}]
      }
    }]
  }'

# Route to EU West
aws route53 change-resource-record-sets \
  --hosted-zone-id $ZONE_ID \
  --change-batch '{
    "Changes": [{
      "Action": "CREATE",
      "ResourceRecordSet": {
        "Name": "example.com",
        "Type": "A",
        "SetIdentifier": "EU-West",
        "Region": "eu-west-1",
        "TTL": 300,
        "ResourceRecords": [{"Value": "203.0.113.2"}]
      }
    }]
  }'

# US users → US-East (low latency)
# EU users → EU-West (low latency)
# Improves user experience globally
```

### 4. Geolocation Routing

Routes based on user's geographic location.

```bash
# Route US traffic
aws route53 change-resource-record-sets \
  --hosted-zone-id $ZONE_ID \
  --change-batch '{
    "Changes": [{
      "Action": "CREATE",
      "ResourceRecordSet": {
        "Name": "example.com",
        "Type": "A",
        "SetIdentifier": "US-Traffic",
        "GeoLocation": {
          "CountryCode": "US"
        },
        "TTL": 300,
        "ResourceRecords": [{"Value": "203.0.113.1"}]
      }
    }]
  }'

# Route EU traffic
aws route53 change-resource-record-sets \
  --hosted-zone-id $ZONE_ID \
  --change-batch '{
    "Changes": [{
      "Action": "CREATE",
      "ResourceRecordSet": {
        "Name": "example.com",
        "Type": "A",
        "SetIdentifier": "EU-Traffic",
        "GeoLocation": {
          "ContinentCode": "EU"
        },
        "TTL": 300,
        "ResourceRecords": [{"Value": "203.0.113.2"}]
      }
    }]
  }'

# Use Case: Content restrictions (geo-blocking)
# Use Case: Localization (different servers per region)
# Use Case: Compliance (data residency requirements)
```

### 5. Geoproximity Routing

Routes based on geographic proximity and bias.

```bash
# Route with bias towards US
aws route53 change-resource-record-sets \
  --hosted-zone-id $ZONE_ID \
  --change-batch '{
    "Changes": [{
      "Action": "CREATE",
      "ResourceRecordSet": {
        "Name": "example.com",
        "Type": "A",
        "SetIdentifier": "US-Biased",
        "GeoProximityLocation": {
          "Latitude": "37.77",
          "Longitude": "-122.41",
          "Bias": 50
        },
        "TTL": 300,
        "ResourceRecords": [{"Value": "203.0.113.1"}]
      }
    }]
  }'

# Bias range: -99 to +99
# Positive bias: Attracts more traffic
# Negative bias: Repels traffic
# Use Case: Shift traffic to newer region gradually
```

### 6. Failover Routing

Active-passive failover with health checks.

```bash
# Primary (Active)
aws route53 change-resource-record-sets \
  --hosted-zone-id $ZONE_ID \
  --change-batch '{
    "Changes": [{
      "Action": "CREATE",
      "ResourceRecordSet": {
        "Name": "example.com",
        "Type": "A",
        "SetIdentifier": "Primary",
        "Failover": "PRIMARY",
        "TTL": 300,
        "ResourceRecords": [{"Value": "203.0.113.1"}],
        "HealthCheckId": "health-check-1"
      }
    }]
  }'

# Secondary (Standby)
aws route53 change-resource-record-sets \
  --hosted-zone-id $ZONE_ID \
  --change-batch '{
    "Changes": [{
      "Action": "CREATE",
      "ResourceRecordSet": {
        "Name": "example.com",
        "Type": "A",
        "SetIdentifier": "Secondary",
        "Failover": "SECONDARY",
        "TTL": 300,
        "ResourceRecords": [{"Value": "203.0.113.2"}]
      }
    }]
  }'

# Primary is healthy: All traffic → Primary
# Primary fails: All traffic → Secondary
# Automatic failover, zero downtime
```

### 7. Multi-Value Answer Routing

Returns multiple healthy IPs.

```bash
aws route53 change-resource-record-sets \
  --hosted-zone-id $ZONE_ID \
  --change-batch '{
    "Changes": [{
      "Action": "CREATE",
      "ResourceRecordSet": {
        "Name": "example.com",
        "Type": "A",
        "SetIdentifier": "Server-1",
        "MultiValueAnswer": true,
        "TTL": 300,
        "ResourceRecords": [{"Value": "203.0.113.1"}],
        "HealthCheckId": "health-check-1"
      }
    }, {
      "Action": "CREATE",
      "ResourceRecordSet": {
        "Name": "example.com",
        "Type": "A",
        "SetIdentifier": "Server-2",
        "MultiValueAnswer": true,
        "TTL": 300,
        "ResourceRecords": [{"Value": "203.0.113.2"}],
        "HealthCheckId": "health-check-2"
      }
    }]
  }'

# Query returns: [203.0.113.1, 203.0.113.2]
# Browser picks randomly
# Unhealthy targets filtered out
# Similar to ALB but at DNS level
```

